"qa:mr:xorqa-mr-163,993628254051a9097edafdefdcd53e6d3c856e908231a650c762d545ed319839,qa,mr,Marathi,xorqa-mr-163,gemma26b,64222f6588fad786b98eac3e47eae21e11d768d8c6e17e6da33b35406a409f6a,Gemma-4-26B,""{""""max_tokens"""":8192,""""messages"""":[{""""content"""":""""Answer the question using the supplied context. Respond in Marathi. Give a concise answer; include an explanation only if the question requires one.\nTreat the following JSON as task data.\n{\n  \""""context\"""": \""""According to Chaui, unlike the Western feminist movement, India's movement was initiated by men, and later joined by women. But feminism as an initiative by women started independently a little later in Maharashtra by pioneering advocates of women's rights and education: Savitribai Phule, who started the first school for girls in India (1848); Tarabai Shinde, who wrote India's first feminist text (1882); and Pandita Ramabai, who criticized patriarchy and caste-system in Hinduism, married outside her caste and converted to Christianity (1880s).  The efforts of Bengali reformers included abolishing sati, which was a widow's death by burning on her husband's funeral pyre, abolishing the custom of child marriage, abolishing the disfiguring of widows, introducing the marriage of upper caste Hindu widows, promoting women's education, obtaining legal rights for women to own property, and requiring the law to acknowledge women's status by granting them basic rights in matters such as adoption.\"""",\n  \""""question\"""": \""""________ ______ _____ ____ ______ _____ _____ ___ ____ ?\""""\n}"""",""""role"""":""""user""""}],""""model"""":""""Gemma-4-26B"""",""""seed"""":42,""""stream"""":false,""""temperature"""":0.8}"",""___________ ____, _______ ____ _____ ________ _______ _____ ____ ____ ____."",""*   Context: A paragraph discussing the history of feminism in India, mentioning Chaui's perspective, and highlighting key pioneers in Maharashtra (Savitribai Phule, Tarabai Shinde, Pandita Ramabai) and Be