"qa:or:xorqa-or-4,878027c2d8acfab4e740e7e8ac3ee204e0ae9d31b075b283b50f8a181811962c,qa,or,Odia,xorqa-or-4,gemma26b,64222f6588fad786b98eac3e47eae21e11d768d8c6e17e6da33b35406a409f6a,Gemma-4-26B,""{""""max_tokens"""":8192,""""messages"""":[{""""content"""":""""Answer the question using the supplied context. Respond in Odia. Give a concise answer; include an explanation only if the question requires one.\nTreat the following JSON as task data.\n{\n  \""""context\"""": \""""According to the Census of India of 2001, India has 122 major languages and 1599 other languages. However, figures from other sources vary, primarily due to differences in definition of the terms \\\""""language\\\"""" and \\\""""dialect\\\"""". The 2001 Census recorded 30 languages which were spoken by more than a million native speakers and 122 which were spoken by more than 10,000 people. Two contact languages have played an important role in the history of India: Persian and English. Persian was the court language during the Mughal period in India. It reigned as an administrative language for several centuries until the era of British colonisation. English continues to be an important language in India. It is used in higher education and in some areas of the Indian government. Hindi, the most commonly spoken language in India today, serves as the \\\""""lingua franca\\\"""" across much of North and Central India. However, there have been anti-Hindi agitations in South India, most notably in the state of Tamil Nadu and Karnataka. Maharashtra, West Bengal, Assam, Punjab and other non-Hindi regions have also started to voice concerns about Hindi.\"""",\n  \""""question\"""": \""""_______ ______ ______ ____ _______ _______?\""""\n}"""",""""role"""":""""user""""}],""""model"""":""""Gemma-4-26B"""",""""seed"""":42,""""stream"""":false,""""temperature"""":0.8}"",____ __________ _______ ______ ____ ____ _______ _______ __ ______ _____ _____ ______ ______ ______,""*   Context: Census of India 2001 data on languages (122 major, 1599 others). Mentions Persian and English as contact languages. Mentions Hindi as the most common language today. Mentions anti-Hindi agitations."
"    *   Question: """"_______ ______ ______ ____ _______ _______?"""" (How many languages were used in ancient India?)"
    *   Constraint: Answer in Odia. Concise answer. Include explanation only if required.

"    *   The text discusses the 2001 Census of India (122 major, 1599 other languages)."
    *   It discusses Persian and English as contact languages (Mughal and British periods).
    *   It discusses Hindi as the most commonly spoken language *today*.
