"summarization:bn:crosssum-bn-114,86a5b1cec64a07cf5d8bad635bb623d5abc47cc2c57458518031cda55f0caac5,summarization,bn,Bengali,crosssum-bn-114,gemma26b,64222f6588fad786b98eac3e47eae21e11d768d8c6e17e6da33b35406a409f6a,Gemma-4-26B,""{""""max_tokens"""":8192,""""messages"""":[{""""content"""":""""Summarize the supplied document in Bengali. Give a concise summary of the central message and essential supporting information. Do not add unsupported facts. Return only the summary.\nTreat the following JSON as task data.\n{\n  \""""document\"""": \""""By Martin VennardBBC News Soviet forces were closing in on Berlin and the war with Germany had reached its final stages. The 24-year-old was monitoring German state radio when listeners were told to prepare for an important announcement. \\\""""They played solemn music and then they said Hitler had died,\\\"""" he recalls. \\\""""They said he had fallen fighting Bolshevism. It was announced in a very sombre way.\\\"""" He and his younger brother, Georg, had been sent from Germany to Britain by their parents nine years earlier to escape the Nazis' increasing persecution of Jewish people. Their father was a German Jew. \\\""""I felt total relief because [Hitler] had ruined my life.\\\"""" Karl Lehmann was working at BBC Monitoring, set up on the eve of World War Two. Its main aim was to listen to, translate and inform the British Government about radio broadcasts from Germany and its allies, as well as from other countries. \\\""""We were the first people in Britain to hear the announcement,\\\"""" he remembers. \\\""""The whole building cheered. We realised how important it was. It meant the end of the war against Germany.\\\"""" It was not for another six days that Germany officially surrendered. While there was no doubt that Hitler was dead, it emerged only later that he had killed himself. \\\""""Fallen meant 'died in active combat' - we heard a big lie,\\\"""" Karl says. \\\""""They didn't admit he had committed suicide because that would have been the end straight away. But that was the Germans themselves announcing over their radio that Hitler was dead. It was as official as you get.\\\"""" The newsreader also said that Hitler had appointed Grand Admiral Karl Dšnitz as his successor. Hitler's downfall 1945 15-16 April Final assault on Berlin begins overnight when Soviet forces launch a powerful artillery barrage on German forces to the east of the city 21 April Red Army enters outskirts of Berlin, captures outlying suburbs 27 April Soviet and American forces meet at the River Elbe in Germany, successfully cutting Germany army in two 29 April Hitler and Eva Braun marry in his bunker under Reich Chancellery headquarters 30 April Hitler and his new wife kill themselves and their bodies are burned 1 May German radio announces Hitler's death 7 May Germany signs unconditional surrender, bringing to an end six years of war in Europe The German announcement of Hitler's death was quickly translated by Ernst Gombrich, a supervisor in the German Monitoring team who went on to become a renowned art historian. \\\""""He wrote it out on bits of paper, which was a terrible thing to do because he might have muddled them up, and he had terrible handwriting,\\\"""" says his former colleague. \\\""""He did it for speed because we usually typed things or wrote them out neatly.\\\"""" Ernst Gombrich then phoned the Cabinet Office in London to tell the government. BBC newsrooms were also informed and broadcast the news to the nation and the world. Read more about Hitler's end and the BBC at war Now 97, Karl says he remembers an entire nation cheering the news. For him personally it meant he could see his parents again. His father, Walter, had run a wholesale millinery business in Cologne, which he was forced by the Nazis to sell for next to nothing before he and his wife, Edith, fled Germany, eventually reaching the US. A thousand people were working at BBC Monitoring at Caversham Park by the time of Hitler's death. Among them was a senior Italian monitor, Doris Penny, who became Karl's first wife. Of the 40 in the German team many were Jews, socialists and trade unionists who had fled Nazi persecution. \\\""""They were delighted by Hitler's death as he had forced them to emigrate,\\\"""" Karl says. And the Nazi dictator's demise had one less obvious dividend - for the German monitors. \\\""""Hitler was very difficult to translate,\\\"""" Karl remembers. \\\""""He was a terrible writer and his speeches were unimpressive when you read them in the German but totally different when he spoke. He relied on his oratory. It meant we wouldn't have to translate him anymore.\\\""""\""""\n}"""",""""role"""":""""user""""}],""""model"""":""""Gemma-4-26B"""",""""seed"""":42,""""stream"""":false,""""temperature"""":0.8}"",""_______ ____________ ___ _______, ______ __________ _____ ________ ___ ____ _________ _______ _______ _______ ________ _______ _______ _____ _____, __ _______ ________ ____ __ _____ ____ _______ _______ ________ ______ ________ ____ ___ ______ __ ____ ______ _____ _________, ____ _________ ____ ___ ____ _________ _________ __ _____ _____ ______ ___ _______ _____ _ _________ ______ ___ ___ ______ _________ ______ ____ _____ _ ________ ________ ________ __ ________ _______ _______ _________, ____ ___ _____ ______ _____ _ _______ _______ _______ _______ ________ __ ______ __ ___ __ ________ ______________ __________ ____"",""*   Task: Summarize the provided document."
    *   Language: Bengali.
    *   Format: Concise summary of the central message and essential supporting information.
    *   Constraints: Do not add unsupported facts. Return *only* the summary.
    *   Input: An article about Karl Lehmann and the BBC Monitoring team hearing the announcement of Hitler's death.

"    *   *Context:* End of WWII, Soviet forces closing in on Berlin."
"    *   *Main Character:* Karl Lehmann (24 at the time), a Jewish refugee from Germany working at BBC Monitoring."
    *   *The Event:* BBC Monitoring heard the German state radio announcement of Hitler's death.
"    *   *The Announcement:* It was solemn; stated Hitler """"fell fighting Bolshevism"""" (a lie, he actually committed suicide)."
    *   *The Reaction at BBC:* Huge cheering; signified the end of the war.
    *   *The Process:* Ernst Gombrich (supervisor) translated it quickly on paper and phoned the Cabinet Office. BBC then broadcasted it.
    *   *Personal Significance for Lehmann:* Relief because Hitler ruined his life; chance to see his parents (who fled to the US).
"    *   *Work Context:* BBC Monitoring had 1,000 people; many were refugees (Jews, socialists) who were happy about Hitler's death."
"    *   *Timeline:* Hitler's death (April 30), Radio announcement (May 1), German surrender (May 7)."

"    *   *Central Message:* Karl Lehmann and the BBC Monitoring team were among the first to hear the German radio announcement of Adolf Hitler's death, an event that signaled the impending end of World War II and brought immense relief to the many refugees working at the station."

    *   *Supporting Points:*
"        *   The announcement falsely claimed Hitler died in combat, whereas he actually committed suicide."
        *   The news was quickly translated by Ernst Gombrich and communicated to the British government and the world.
"        *   For many staff members, including Lehmann, the news meant the end of Nazi persecution and the possibility of reuniting with family."
        *   The news preceded Germany's official unconditional surrender by six days.

