"summarization:mr:crosssum-mr-25,3d756346ef17d2f74310fd78eedf9b5c07a7077f573637e08b13ee490d5665e9,summarization,mr,Marathi,crosssum-mr-25,gemma26b,64222f6588fad786b98eac3e47eae21e11d768d8c6e17e6da33b35406a409f6a,Gemma-4-26B,""{""""max_tokens"""":8192,""""messages"""":[{""""content"""":""""Summarize the supplied document in Marathi. Give a concise summary of the central message and essential supporting information. Do not add unsupported facts. Return only the summary.\nTreat the following JSON as task data.\n{\n  \""""document\"""": \""""Prof Thaler, of Chicago Booth business school, co-wrote the global best seller Nudge, which looked at how people make bad or irrational choices. Judges said he had demonstrated how \\\""""nudging\\\"""" - a term he coined - may help people to exercise better self-control. He will receive 9 million Swedish krona (£850,000) from the committee. \\\""""I will try to spend it as irrationally as possible!\\\"""" the 72 year-old economist said. Nudging Prof Thaler's work led to the UK setting up a \\\""""nudge unit\\\"""" under former prime minister David Cameron. It was launched in 2010 to find innovative ways of changing public behaviour and has offices in the UK, New York, Singapore and Sydney. One of the Nobel prize judges, Per Stroemberg, said Prof Thaler's work had explored how human psychology shaped economic decisions. \\\""""Richard Thaler's findings have inspired many other researchers coming in his footsteps and it has paved the way for a new field in economics which we call behavioural economics,\\\"""" Mr Stroemberg said. The panel said Prof Thaler's insights helped people to recognise marketing tricks and avoid bad economic decisions. In particular, his work looked at how to \\\""""nudge\\\"""" people into doing more long-term planning, such as saving for a pension. Prof Thaler also made a cameo appearance in the Hollywood film, The Big Short, explaining the complex financial instruments that led to the financial crisis of 2007 and 2008. Americans dominate It is the final Nobel to be announced this year, after prizes for medicine, physics, chemistry, literature and peace were awarded last week. The economics prize is the only Nobel not created by Alfred Nobel, and was instead launched in 1968, long after the philanthropist's death. To date the US has dominated the prize, with American economists accounting for roughly half of laureates since it started. Between 2000 and 2013, US academics won or shared the prize every year. Last year, UK-born Oliver Hart and Bengt Holmstrom of Finland won the award - officially called the Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of Alfred Nobel - for their work on contract theory. Analysis: How 'nudge' theory works By Kamal Ahmed, BBC economics editor Prof Thaler's central insight is that we are not the rational beings beloved of more traditional economic theory. Given two options, we are likely to pick the wrong one even if that means making ourselves less well off. Lack of thinking time, habit and poor decision making mean that even when presented with a factual analysis (for example on healthy eating) we are still likely to pick burger and chips. We're hungry, we're in a hurry and burger and chips is what we always buy. Nudge theory takes account of this, based as it is on the simple premise that people will often choose what is easiest over what is wisest. Tests have shown that putting healthier foods on a higher shelf increases sales. The food is more likely to be in someone's eye line and therefore \\\""""nudge\\\"""" that person towards the purchase - whether they had any idea about the obesity argument or not. Read more from Kamal's blog. Previous winners of the Nobel prize for economics:\""""\n}"""",""""role"""":""""user""""}],""""model"""":""""Gemma-4-26B"""",""""seed"""":42,""""stream"""":false,""""temperature"""":0.8}"",""________ ______ ____ _____ '__________ __________' (behavioral economics) ___ '__' (Nudge) ____________ _________ ____________ ___________ _______________ _____ ________ ____ ________ _______ ___ ___. _____ ______ ______ ________ (rational) ____ ___ ___ ______ ________ _____ ________ ___________ _________ ________ _______ ______ _____, __ _______ _____ ____ ___. '_____' (Nudging) ____________ _______ _________ ______ ______ (___. __________ ___ ____) _______ ________ ________ ____ ____. _________ __ __________ _________ _______ _________ ___ _____ __________ _____ __________ '__ _______' ______ _______ _____ ____."",""*   Task: Summarize the provided document."
    *   Language: Marathi.
    *   Format: Concise summary of the central message and essential supporting information.
    *   Constraints: Do not add unsupported facts. Return *only* the summary.
"    *   Input: A text about Professor Richard Thaler winning the Nobel Prize in Economics for his work on behavioral economics and """"nudge"""" theory."

    *   *Subject:* Professor Richard Thaler (Chicago Booth business school).
    *   *Achievement:* Won the Nobel Prize in Economics (Sveriges Riksbank Prize).
"    *   *Core Concept:* """"Nudge"""" theory / Behavioral Economics."
"    *   *Key Findings:* People are not always rational; they make suboptimal choices due to habits, lack of time, or psychological factors."
"    *   *Impact of """"Nudge"""":* Helps people with self-control (e.g., saving for pensions), helps recognize marketing tricks, used by governments (UK """"nudge unit"""") to change public behavior."
    *   *Example:* Placing healthy food at eye level to encourage better choices.
    *   *Context:* The Economics Nobel is the last one announced this year. US dominance in this prize is noted.

"    *   *Central Message:* Professor Richard Thaler won the Nobel Prize in Economics for his work on behavioral economics and the """"nudge"""" theory."
    *   *Supporting Info 1:* He showed that humans are often irrational and make choices based on ease rather than wisdom.
"    *   *Supporting Info 2:* """"Nudging"""" can guide people toward better decisions, like saving for retirement, without forcing them."
"    *   *Supporting Info 3:* This work has led to the creation of """"nudge units"""" in governments to influence public behavior positively."

    *   *Central Message:* ______ ___ ______ _______ __________ ______ ____ _____ _________ '__' (Nudge) _____________ ________________ _____ ________ ____ ________ _______ ___ ___. (Professor Richard Thaler of Chicago Booth Business School has been honored with the Nobel Prize in Economics for his 'Nudge' theory.)
"    *   *Details:* _______ '__________ __________' (behavioral economics) __ ____ _________ ______ ______ ____ ___. _____ ______ ______ ________ (rational) ____ ___ ___ ______ ______ ______ _____, __ _______ _____ ____. '_____' (Nudging) ____________ _______ _________ ________, ___ __ __________ ___ ____, _____ ______ _______ ___ ____ ____. _________ __ __________ _____________ _______ _________ _______ ___ _____ __________ '__ _______' ______ ____ ____. (They have made significant contributions to the new field of behavioral economics. They proved that human nature is not always rational and people often make wrong decisions. Through 'nudging' technique, people can be helped to make the right decisions for their benefit, such as saving for a pension. Due to their work, countries like the UK have established 'nudge units' to bring changes in public behavior.)"

    *   *Constraint Check:* Is it concise? Yes. Does it include unsupported facts? No. Is it in Marathi? Yes. Is it *only* the summary? Yes.

"    ________ ______ ____ _____ '__________ __________' (behavioral economics) ___ '__' (Nudge) ____________ _________ ____________ ___________ _______________ _____ ________ ____ ________ _______ ___ ___. _____ ______ ______ ________ (rational) ____ ___ ___ ______ ________ _____ ________ ___________ _________ ________ _______ ______ _____, __ _______ _____ ____. '_____' ____________ _______ _________ ______ ______ (___. __________ ___ ____) _______ ________ ________ ____ ____. _________ __ __________ _________ _______ _________ ___ _____ __________ _____ __________ '__ _______' ______ _______ _____ ____."

