    *   Concise summary of the central message and essential supporting information.
    *   No unsupported facts.
    *   Return *only* the summary.
*   Input format: JSON.

    *   *Discovery:* A Jupiter-like giant planet (GJ 3512b) orbiting a small M-type red dwarf star (GJ 3512).
"    *   *Significance:* It contradicts current theories of planet formation. Usually, small stars (red dwarfs) are thought to only form small planets (like Earth or Super-Earths) because their gas/dust discs lack enough material for core accretion."
"    *   *The Conflict:* Core accretion (the traditional theory) suggests giant planets form far out and grow slowly. However, small stars don't have enough material for this."
"    *   *The New Theory:* Gravitational instability/collapse. A part of the disc collapses due to its own gravity, forming a planet suddenly. This is more likely for small stars."
    *   *Specifics of GJ 3512b:* Mass is about half of Jupiter's. Orbit is eccentric (oval-shaped). It likely migrated from further out.
    *   *Implication:* Scientists need to review their models of planet formation.

"    *   *Central Message:* ______________ ___ _____ __________ ________________ (GJ 3512b), ___ ___ _____ _______ ____________ (red dwarf) _______________ ______ ________. ___ _________ _________ ____ _______________ ______ ______. (Astronomers have found a new planet (GJ 3512b) orbiting a small red dwarf star. This challenges old theories of planet formation.)"

"    *   *Supporting Info 1 (The anomaly):* ________ _____ ______________ _____ ___________ (____ __________) ___________ _____ ______________. _____, ______ _____ _______ __________ _____ ___ _____________ __________ ___________. (Usually, small stars are thought to form small planets. But this star has a giant planet like Jupiter.)"

"    *   *Supporting Info 2 (The theory):* '____ ________' (Core accretion) _________ _______ _____ ________________ _______ _____ _________ ______ ________. ________ ______, _______ __________ _______ ____ _______ ________ ________ _____ (gravitational collapse) _____ ________ __________________ _____ ___________ _____________. (According to core accretion, such large planets can't form around small stars. Instead, they might have formed via sudden gravitational collapse of the disc.)"

"    *   *Supporting Info 3 (The orbit/migration):* ____ __________ ____________ ___________ (eccentric), ___ ___ ____ ______ _________ ____ ________ _______ ________ ______________ ________ ___________. (The orbit is eccentric, suggesting it migrated from far away.)"

