        *   Concise summary of the central message and essential supporting information.
        *   No unsupported facts.
        *   Return *only* the summary.
    *   Input: A text about a rare inflammatory syndrome (PIMS-TS) in children potentially linked to COVID-19.

    *   *Subject:* A rare inflammatory syndrome in children (PIMS-TS).
"    *   *Symptoms:* High fever, rash, red eyes, swelling, general pain, swollen glands, dry/cracked lips, heart/circulation issues (some need ventilators)."
"    *   *Demographics:* Affects children (up to age 16), unlike Kawasaki disease which is mostly under 5."
    *   *Link to COVID-19:* Occurs 3-6 weeks after a COVID-19 peak; likely a delayed immune response (antibody-related) rather than active infection (many test negative for virus but positive for antibodies).
"    *   *Prevalence:* Very rare (up to 100 in UK; similar cases in US, Europe, etc.)."
    *   *Severity:* Some need intensive care/ventilators; most respond well to treatment.
    *   *Current Status:* Doctors/researchers are investigating it globally to understand why it happens.

"    *   *Central Message:* A rare inflammatory syndrome (PIMS-TS) is affecting children, likely as a delayed immune response to COVID-19."
    *   *Key Details:*
"        *   Symptoms: Fever, rash, swelling, and sometimes heart issues."
"        *   Mechanism: It's a post-infectious phenomenon (antibodies present, virus often not found)."
"        *   Global presence: Seen in UK, US, and Europe."
"        *   Severity: Most recover, but some need intensive care."

