"summarization:te:crosssum-te-108,520443e163b5bf6f3b6a0f8515c23cf90f1cf17a095510ba03796e08e5a4ff46,summarization,te,Telugu,crosssum-te-108,gemma26b,64222f6588fad786b98eac3e47eae21e11d768d8c6e17e6da33b35406a409f6a,Gemma-4-26B,""{""""max_tokens"""":8192,""""messages"""":[{""""content"""":""""Summarize the supplied document in Telugu. Give a concise summary of the central message and essential supporting information. Do not add unsupported facts. Return only the summary.\nTreat the following JSON as task data.\n{\n  \""""document\"""": \""""Haqqani died after a long illness and was buried in Afghanistan, the sources added. Rumours about Haqqani's death have circulated for some years and can still not be independently confirmed. The latest report comes a day after the Taliban acknowledged that its leader, Mullah Omar, was dead. Reports of Haqqani's death, quoting Taliban sources, also appeared in Pakistani media on Friday. One senior Afghan official said he had died six years ago. The network has never confirmed the death of its founder. A man linked to the family denied Friday's reports, telling the BBC that Jalaluddin Haqqani was still alive but ill. The Haqqani network - based in the tribal regions of Pakistan with links to al-Qaeda and the Taliban - has been behind many of the co-ordinated attacks on Afghan and Nato forces in recent years. Haqqani's son, Sirajuddin Haqqani, has long been thought to be in de facto control of the group and has just been announced as a deputy leader of the Taliban. Tribal regions Jalaluddin Haqqani was an Afghan guerrilla leader who fought Soviet troops that occupied Afghanistan in 1980s. US officials have admitted that at the time he was a prized asset of the Central Intelligence Agency (CIA). However, he later allied himself to the Taliban after they took power in Afghanistan in 1996. Haqqani served as a cabinet minister under the Taliban's supreme leader, Mullah Omar. The Haqqani network was one of several militant groups that operated from the tribal areas along the Afghan-Pakistan border following the US-led military campaign in Afghanistan, which began in 2001. BBC regional analyst Charles Haviland says Jalaluddin Haqqani turned against the West with a vengeance, allowing his base in Pakistan to be used by militants who inflicted huge casualties in Afghanistan, including among Western troops. As the Haqqanis grew in strength, Pakistan's security establishment was accused of secretly supporting the group, although it has strongly denied this. Analysts say the network has always been part of the Taliban and its members accepted Mullah Omar as their leader. On Thursday the Taliban said they had appointed their deputy leader, Mullah Akhtar Mansour, as successor to Mullah Omar. Correspondents say the move is likely to divide the group and that many senior figures opposed the appointment.\""""\n}"""",""""role"""":""""user""""}],""""model"""":""""Gemma-4-26B"""",""""seed"""":42,""""stream"""":false,""""temperature"""":0.8}"",""___________ _______ _________. ___ _______ _____________ _____________ _____ ________ ____ ______. _______ __________ ___________ _______ ________ ________ ___, _____ CIA _______ ________, ____ ______ __________ ______ _______. _______ _____ ____ ______ ______ ____ _______ __________, ___-____ _____ __________ ______ _____ ____. ___ ________ ____________ _______ _________ ________ ________ ________ _________________."",""*   Task: Summarize the provided document."
    *   Language: Telugu.
    *   Requirements:
        *   Concise summary of the central message and essential supporting information.
        *   No unsupported facts.
        *   Return *only* the summary.
"    *   Input Data: A text about the death of Jalaluddin Haqqani, his background, the Haqqani network, and the political context (Taliban leadership)."

    *   *Main Event:* Jalaluddin Haqqani has died after a long illness and was buried in Afghanistan (according to sources).
    *   *Context of Death:* Rumors have existed for years; previous reports suggested he died years ago; his family/links denied it recently claiming he was alive but ill.
    *   *Who is Jalaluddin Haqqani?* Afghan guerrilla leader against Soviets (was CIA asset); later allied with Taliban; served as a minister under Mullah Omar.
    *   *Haqqani Network:* Based in Pakistan's tribal regions; links to al-Qaeda and Taliban; responsible for many attacks on Afghan/NATO forces; suspected (but denied by Pakistan) support from Pakistan's security establishment.
"    *   *Succession/Family:* His son, Sirajuddin Haqqani, is thought to be in control and was recently named deputy leader of the Taliban."
"    *   *Taliban Context:* Mullah Omar is dead; Mullah Akhtar Mansour is the new successor, which might cause division."

