    *   *Main Topic:* Scientists (Roslin Technologies/University of Edinburgh) have found a way to produce drugs (proteins) more cheaply using genetically modified chickens.
    *   *Mechanism:* They insert a human gene (for a specific protein) into the DNA responsible for egg white production.
    *   *Cost-Effectiveness:* 10 to 100 times cheaper than factory production because chicken sheds are cheaper than sterile clean rooms.
    *   *Key Proteins:* IFNalpha2a (antiviral/anti-cancer) and macrophage-CSF (tissue repair).
    *   *Efficiency:* 3 eggs can produce one dose; chickens lay ~300 eggs/year.
"    *   *Applications:* Human medicine (though it takes 10-20 years for regulation), animal health (boosting immune systems to reduce antibiotic use), and pet medicine (organ regeneration)."
"    *   *Status:* Currently for research, not for supermarket sale. Commercially viable for drug discovery."
    *   *Ethical Note:* Chickens live comfortably and are not harmed.

